+VERSION: 1
+COMPONENTS: engine/orchestrator/run
+
+disc-1  PROVISIONAL CHOICES, TAKEN TO BUILD, NOT RULED       ** OPEN **
+______________________________________________________________________________
+
+ 1  (ruled: O-11 -- directories run in parallel; '--dbd' serial.)
+
+ 2  EVENT 'directory' FIELDS ARE ROOT-RELATIVE, '/'-separated; the
+    walk's own faults carry '.'. No event carries a machine-chosen
+    path -- which also keeps the streams blessable.
+
+ 3  'tree-done.good' = every directory good AND no fault anywhere,
+    walk faults included. 'fail_n' counts failed NODES, tree-wide.
+
+ 4  THE CLOSING SENTINEL is one 'None' after 'tree-done'.
+
+ 5  THE CLOCK is injectable ('clock=' on CTreeScheduler and
+    orchestrator); the default is UTC now, seconds resolution.
+
+ 6  'orchestrator()' requires a RUNNING LOOP and uses
+    asyncio.ensure_future; it does not create a loop of its own.
+
+ 7  THE FACTORY takes (directory, entry) -- the entry's app_set is
+    what the real dispatcher resolves nodes against; a double ignores
+    it.
+ 8  FRAME_CAPS: frame scripts run under a fixed 60 s procsitter cap;
+    a directory-level word for it is unruled.
+ 9  'test-app-contained' is the report of a choice whose session fell
+    before serving it -- the mapping ChoiceExecute always spoke;
+    whether a finer word ('session-ended' as its own E_TestRunResult)
+    is warranted is unruled.
+10  A directory whose dispatcher construction or run RAISES emits
+    'fault' + 'dir-done good=false' and the tree walks on -- the
+    stream never hangs on a broken directory.
diff --git a/vut/adm/WORK/gathered/0a-disc-7-caps-per-choice.txt b/vut/adm/WORK/gathered/0a-disc-7-caps-per-choice.txt
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/vut/adm/WORK/gathered/0a-disc-7-caps-per-choice.txt
@@ -0,0 +1,67 @@
+VERSION: 1
+COMPONENTS: engine/orchestrator/run
+
+disc-7   CAPS ARE ACCEPTED PER CHOICE AND IGNORED PER CHOICE
+         (opened 2026-09-02, found while settling C-6)
+
+THE FACTS, measured
+
+  The header vocabulary ADMITS 'caps' inside a choice:
+
+      choices {
+          massive-pipe { caps { memory_mb = 2048 } }
+          soduko       { caps { memory_mb = 2048 } }
+      }
+
+  the validator accepts it, and 'hwut.show' PRINTS it per choice --
+  two choices at 512, two at 2048, exactly as written.
+
+  The run ignores it. 'adapter.test_configuration_of' reads
+
+      caps = _caps_of(root)                      # ONE value
+
+  and 'TestConfiguration.caps' is a single ProcsitterConfig for the
+  whole application; 'TestChoiceConfiguration' holds no caps at all.
+  So the stated cap never reaches procsitter, and the choice runs
+  under the root's.
+
+  Worse, with the SCOPE form of 'choices' and no root statement there
+  is no 'None' choice, so '_root_of' answers THE FIRST CHOICE BY NAME
+  ('backtracking' here) -- and every other choice silently runs under
+  the caps of whichever choice sorts first.
+
+WHY IT MATTERS
+
+  A parameter that is stated, validated, displayed and then ignored is
+  worse than one that is refused: 'hwut.show' becomes a document that
+  lies, and the author has no way to learn it. C-6 had to state the
+  cap at the application root instead, raising it for two choices that
+  do not need it.
+
+THE FORK
+
+  (a)  CAPS BECOME PER CHOICE
+       'TestChoiceConfiguration' gains 'caps'; the session takes the
+       choice's where stated and the application's else, as every
+       other choice parameter already works.
+       +  the vocabulary means what it says
+       +  a stress choice is capped as a stress choice, its siblings
+          are not
